// The parts of a hand-written parser that every grammar rewrites.
//
// A program that has to read a config format, a wire text format or a source language starts at the
// byte cursor and builds the same four things every time: a scanner, a span, literal scanning with
// its escape rules, and a way to say where the mistake was. This package is those four, plus the
// two that are fiddly enough to be worth writing once -- the layout pass an indentation-sensitive
// grammar needs, and the binding-power loop an expression grammar needs.
//
// **It is not a parser generator and not a combinator library.** A grammar stays hand-written
// recursive descent, which is what Clang, rustc, Go, TypeScript, Swift, Zig and javac all are; what
// this removes is the four hundred lines underneath it that are the same in all of them.
//
// This is the block a *consumer* writes to reach this package:
//
//     dependencies {
//       parsing { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/parsing", version = "0.5.0" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate names the package, `parsing`; the module a program imports is `sh.sysl.parsing`.
// The module carries the `sh.sysl.` prefix so that no package claims a top-level word another one
// might want, and the package name is the short one because it names this repository rather than a
// namespace.

package {
  name    = "parsing"
  version = "0.5.0"

  // **The oldest compiler this builds with, and it is a released one.** Three of 0.0.76's changes
  // are written into these files rather than merely available to them: a `match` standing as an
  // argument in `tests.sysl`, a branch that takes its type from its sibling in `diag.sysl`,
  // `layout.sysl` and `tokens.sysl`, and `sysl.text.parse_real` reading a `[]const u8` in
  // `literal.sysl`. The first is a parse error on an older compiler and the last is an unresolved
  // call, so the floor is stated here rather than left to a reader to work out from a diagnostic.
  //
  // **This package is what raised all three.** Writing it is what found them, which is the argument
  // for a package existing at all: the toolkit is the first thing in the org that reads text for a
  // living, so it is the first thing to notice what reading text is awkward to write.
  sysl = "0.0.76"
}

// **0.5.0 -- THE READERS A GRAMMAR NEEDS WHEN ITS LITERALS ARE NOT C'S.**
//
// Four additions, all raised by writing `sysl-lang/toml` and none of them incompatible. The theme is
// the one this package has had from the start: a grammar whose escapes or quotes differ from the C
// family's could not call `read_escape` or `read_quoted` at all, so it wrote the fiddly part itself
// -- and the fiddly part is where the surrogate test and the delimiter run get left out.
//
// - **`read_hex_escape`** is `read_escape`'s own machinery, made public: exactly `n` hexadecimal
//   digits, and the character they name, refusing a surrogate and anything past U+10FFFF.
// - **`read_raw`** is `read_quoted` with no escapes at all -- TOML's `'…'`, Rust's `r"…"`, a Windows
//   path, a regular expression.
// - **`fence_run`** answers what a run of the delimiter means inside a `"""…"""` literal, which is
//   the rule every implementation gets wrong in the same direction: up to two of the delimiter may
//   belong to the text, so `"""he said """"` is `he said "`. It consumes nothing.
// - **`\U`** in `read_escape`, which is C's other universal character name and was the one escape
//   missing from a set this package says is the C family's. Nothing was silently wrong -- it was
//   refused as unknown -- it simply could not be written.
//
// **0.4.0 -- A LITERAL WITH EXPRESSIONS WRITTEN INTO IT, AND A WAY TO RE-READ ONE SLICE OF A FILE.**
//
// Three additions, all of them raised by slate wanting `s"a ${b} c"` and none of them incompatible.
//
// - **`read_interpolated`** reads a quoted literal that has holes in it, answering the chunks with
//   their escapes decoded and the *extents* of the holes -- not what a hole says, which is the
//   grammar's business. What it owns is the part every grammar gets wrong the same way: a `}` inside
//   a nested bracket pair nests, and a `}` inside a *string* inside the hole is not a brace at all,
//   so `s"${ f("}") }"` closes where a reader says it does. The opener and closer are the caller's,
//   so `${`, `#{` and a bare `{` all work, and a doubled sigil is a literal one -- which is Scala's
//   `$$` and Python's `{{` falling out of one line.
// - **`sub_source`** answers a source holding one slice of another **at the offsets it already
//   occupies**: everything outside becomes a space and a newline stays a newline. That is what lets
//   a grammar re-read part of its input -- the inside of an interpolated string, an embedded
//   sub-language, a macro body -- and still have every diagnostic in it name the line, column and
//   character the reader is looking at. Lexing the slice on its own gives spans measured from the
//   slice, which is a caret nobody can find.
// - **`Report.at` and `Report.absorb`.** A grammar that parsed part of its input separately has two
//   reports where the reader should see one. Reaching into `items` from outside was the alternative.
//
// **The seam is where it always is in this package: extents and text here, meaning in the grammar.**

// **0.3.0 -- A BLOCK MAY OPEN INSIDE BRACKETS, WHICH IS THE FEATURE `layout` WAS MISSING.**
//
// A bracket used to suspend the off-side rule outright, so a construct whose body is an indented
// block could not be written as an argument:
//
//     print(n match
//         0 -> "none"
//         1 -> "one")
//
// The arms' newlines were inside a bracket pair and therefore meant nothing, so the block never
// opened. sysl hit exactly this in its own grammar and fixed it in 0.0.76 (card `0248`); slate hit
// it writing a `match` as a call argument, which is what brought it here.
//
// **The mechanism is this module's and the policy is the grammar's.** `opens_block` is how a lexer
// says "the token I just emitted opens a block", and which tokens those are cannot be guessed here:
// sysl's are `match` and `->` and deliberately nothing else, another language's might be `:` or `do`.
// What `layout` now owns is the bookkeeping that makes such a block possible.
//
// Three things changed and two of them are incompatible:
//
// - **`close_bracket` answers how many blocks it closed.** A block opened inside a bracket pair is
//   closed by the `)`, not by a line -- the `)` sits on the same line as the block's last line, so
//   no line ending will ever close it. The count comes back so a lexer can emit those `DEDENT`s
//   before the `)` it is about to emit. A caller that ignored the result needs no change; one that
//   did not emit the dedents will drift.
// - **`Layout.columns` is now `Layout.blocks`, a `Buf[Block]`.** Each open block records the bracket
//   depth it was opened at, which is the whole of how `significant` decides: a newline counts where
//   the bracket depth is the one the innermost block was opened at. That agrees with the old rule
//   everywhere a block sits at the top level, which is why "no brackets are open" served until now.
// - `Tokens.looked_back` answers the token just taken, value and all. `previous` gives its span;
//   this gives what it was, which is what a block-structured grammar asks when deciding whether a
//   statement has ended.

// **0.2.1 -- ONE FIX, AND IT IS WHAT LETS THIS PACKAGE BE USED ON A BARE-METAL TARGET AT ALL.**
// `accept_newline` matched a two-byte slice held in a module-level `val`, and a module-level `val` is
// *module storage*: an initializer fills it before a program's statements run, and a `build-c` archive
// for a freestanding target has no entry point and no loader to run one. So an `@export` that reached
// `accept_newline` -- which is every lexer that recognises a line ending -- was refused outright:
//
//     error: 'boot' is exported and reaches '...parsing.crlf', which is module storage an
//     initializer fills before the program's own statements run ...
//
// It is written as two byte tests now, which needs no storage and is faster. **Found 2026-08-25 by
// ogol's reader**, this package's first consumer on a board, and it had been there since 0.1.0 --
// nothing had looked, because until then nothing here ran anywhere but a host.
//
// **The check is a command and there is no test for it**, because `sysl test .` runs on the host and
// cannot see this. Run it when anything here grows a module-level binding:
//
//     sysl build-c <a program with @export("main") reaching it> --target thumb-freestanding-softfp
//
// **A scalar `val` is fine and was checked rather than assumed** -- `literal.sysl`'s `ulong_max` is
// laid into the object file as constant data, and a program reaching `read_digits` builds. What is not
// fine is anything whose initializer is a *call*: `private const s: ByteSet = byte_set_of("...")` is
// refused as "not a constant expression", so a set a grammar wants belongs in a local or on a struct.

// **0.2.0 rather than 0.1.1, because one thing in it is not compatible.** `Dent.Ragged` carries the
// number of blocks the ragged line closed, where it used to carry nothing -- a grammar matching on
// it has to be changed. Nothing else here moves: the two bug fixes and the sweep are all behaviour a
// caller wanted anyway.

// **`heap = true`, and the reason is one field.** `Source` holds a line-start table, and how many
// lines an input has is not known until it has been read, so the table is a `Buf[usize]`. Everything
// else here allocates nothing: `Scanner` is a slice and an offset, `Span` and `Loc` are two integers
// each, `literal` decodes into a buffer the caller supplies, and `pratt` is a loop over callbacks.
//
// **The alternative was refused deliberately.** The table could have been caller-supplied -- an
// array sized by counting newlines first -- which would have let the package state `requires {}` and
// run on a target with no allocator. What it costs is that every consumer counts newlines twice and
// carries storage whose size it has to have worked out, in order to reach a table that exists for
// the error path. The scanner tier is the part a microcontroller would want and it is the part that
// needs nothing; a program on such a target can use `Scanner` directly against its own bytes and
// never build a `Source`. That is a real cost of this clause and it is being accepted, not fixed:
// today the capability is claimed for the whole package, so such a program cannot depend on it at
// all.
//
// **What is NOT here is `os` or `posix`.** Nothing in this package opens, reads or writes anything.
// A `Source` is made over bytes the caller already has, and a diagnostic renders into a sink the
// caller passes -- so where the input came from and where the report goes are both the program's
// business.
requires {
  heap = true
}
